"""Chapter 2 drill items — money and banking."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_2 = [
    q("Money is best defined as anything that is", ["issued by a government", "generally acceptable as a means of payment", "made of paper or metal", "backed by gold reserves"], 1, "General acceptability is the defining property. Government issue, paper form and metallic backing are characteristics of particular monies, not of money as such."),
    q("The function of money that eliminates the double coincidence of wants is", ["unit of account", "store of value", "medium of exchange", "standard of deferred payment"], 2, "Medium of exchange. Barter requires that each party wants exactly what the other has; money breaks that requirement by being acceptable to everybody."),
    q("When prices are quoted in a single monetary unit, money is serving as a", ["medium of exchange", "unit of account", "store of value", "means of deferred payment"], 1, "Unit of account — the common scale in which values are expressed and accounts kept, which is separate from the act of paying."),
    q("Money's usefulness as a store of value is most seriously weakened by", ["rapid inflation", "a rise in the interest rate", "an increase in the money supply alone", "the use of cheques"], 0, "Inflation. If prices rise quickly, money held over time buys less, so it stores value badly compared with assets whose price rises with inflation."),
    q("The function that makes lending and borrowing possible is money as a", ["medium of exchange", "unit of account", "standard of deferred payment", "measure of value"], 2, "Standard of deferred payment. A debt is fixed in a unit and repaid later, which requires that the unit be expected to exist and be accepted at repayment."),
    q("The quality of money that conflicts most with its function as a store of value is its", ["portability", "divisibility", "liquidity", "durability"], 2, "Liquidity. Cash is the most liquid asset precisely because it can be spent at once, which is also the reason it earns no return and loses value under inflation."),
    q("Which of the following is the most liquid asset?", ["a house", "a fixed deposit", "cash", "government bonds"], 2, "Cash can be exchanged for goods immediately and at no cost, so it is the most liquid. A fixed deposit, a bond and a house each take longer and cost more to convert."),
    q("Legal tender means money that", ["is backed by gold", "must be accepted in settlement of a debt", "is issued by commercial banks", "earns interest"], 1, "Must be accepted in settlement of a debt. That is a legal property conferred by the state, and it has nothing to do with metallic backing or with interest."),
    q("Commercial banks create credit when they", ["print additional currency", "lend out part of the deposits they hold", "borrow from the central bank", "buy treasury bills"], 1, "Lending creates a deposit. Printing currency is the central bank's monopoly, borrowing from the central bank changes the banks' reserves rather than creating credit directly, and buying bills employs existing funds."),
    q("If the cash reserve ratio is 20 per cent, the credit multiplier is", ["0.2", "2", "5", "20"], 2, "1 ÷ 0.20 = 5. The ratio and the multiplier are reciprocals, and confusing them is the commonest error in the topic."),
    q("A bank receives a deposit of ₦20,000 and the reserve ratio is 25 per cent. Total deposits created are", ["₦5,000", "₦20,000", "₦80,000", "₦100,000"], 2, "₦20,000 × (1 ÷ 0.25) = ₦20,000 × 4 = ₦80,000. ₦5,000 is the reserve, ₦20,000 applies no multiplier, and ₦100,000 divides by 0.20 instead of 0.25."),
    q("An increase in the cash reserve ratio will", ["increase bank lending", "reduce bank lending", "leave bank lending unchanged", "increase the money supply"], 1, "Reduce lending: a larger fraction of every deposit must be held idle, so the multiplier falls and fewer loans can be made."),
    q("The credit multiplier is smaller than theory suggests when", ["all borrowers deposit their loans in the same bank", "banks hold excess reserves and the public holds cash", "the reserve ratio is low", "the central bank buys securities"], 1, "Leaks reduce it. Cash held by the public and excess reserves held by banks both stop a loan from returning to the system as a fresh deposit, so the series converges sooner."),
    q("The central bank of a country is described as the lender of last resort because it", ["lends to the government only", "lends to banks that cannot raise funds elsewhere", "lends to the public at the lowest rate", "lends only for long-term projects"], 1, "It lends to a solvent bank in a liquidity crisis. That is what stops a run on one bank becoming a collapse of the system, and it is not about lending to government or to the public."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a function of the central bank?", ["issuing currency", "acting as banker to the government", "accepting deposits from the general public", "managing the external reserves"], 2, "Accepting deposits from the general public is commercial banking. The central bank deals with the government and with banks, not with individuals."),
    q("The bank that holds the accounts of the federal government and manages its debt is the", ["commercial bank", "merchant bank", "central bank", "development bank"], 2, "The central bank, acting as banker to the government. Commercial and merchant banks serve firms and individuals, and a development bank funds specific sectors."),
    q("Open market operations involve the central bank", ["fixing the exchange rate", "buying and selling government securities", "setting the cash reserve ratio", "persuading banks to lend"], 1, "Buying and selling securities to drain or release liquidity. The reserve ratio is a separate instrument, and persuasion is moral suasion."),
    q("To reduce inflation the central bank would", ["buy securities and lower the reserve ratio", "sell securities and raise the reserve ratio", "lower the policy rate", "lend more to commercial banks"], 1, "Sell securities and raise the reserve ratio — both drain liquidity and make credit dearer. The other pair is expansionary and would worsen the inflation."),
    q("The instrument sometimes described as moral suasion is", ["changes in the reserve ratio", "open market operations", "persuasion of banks to follow the central bank's wishes", "changes in the exchange rate"], 2, "Persuasion. It operates through the banks' willingness to cooperate rather than through a price or a rule, which is exactly why it is called moral."),
    q("Inflation is best defined as", ["a rise in the price of one commodity", "a sustained rise in the general price level", "a fall in the value of the currency abroad", "an increase in the money supply"], 1, "A sustained rise in the general price level. A single price rise is a relative price change, and a currency's external value is the exchange rate."),
    q("Inflation caused by an increase in aggregate demand beyond the economy's capacity is called", ["cost-push inflation", "demand-pull inflation", "imported inflation", "structural inflation"], 1, "Demand-pull. Cost-push comes from the supply side, imported inflation arrives through the exchange rate, and structural inflation arises from bottlenecks in particular sectors."),
    q("A sharp rise in the price of imported raw materials is most likely to cause", ["demand-pull inflation", "cost-push inflation", "deflation", "a fall in the price level"], 1, "Cost-push: costs rise, so aggregate supply shifts left, raising prices and reducing output."),
    q("Cost-push inflation is more difficult to cure than demand-pull because", ["it only affects the rich", "the usual anti-inflationary policies also reduce output and employment", "it cannot be measured", "it does not affect the price level"], 1, "Cutting demand lowers prices but also lowers output, which the economy is already short of. Demand-pull inflation can be damped without that same damage."),
    q("During a period of rapid inflation, the group that gains is", ["pensioners on fixed incomes", "holders of fixed-interest bonds", "debtors on fixed-interest loans", "savers with money in current accounts"], 2, "Debtors repay in money worth less than they borrowed. Fixed-income earners, bondholders and savers all receive sums fixed in nominal terms and lose real value."),
    q("Which group suffers most from inflation?", ["borrowers with variable-rate loans", "people on fixed money incomes", "holders of real assets", "traders who can reprice daily"], 1, "Fixed incomes. A pension fixed in naira buys steadily less; borrowers, asset holders and traders able to reprice have some protection."),
    q("Deflation is", ["a fall in the rate of inflation", "a sustained fall in the general price level", "a fall in the value of money", "a rise in the exchange rate"], 1, "A sustained fall in prices. A fall in the inflation rate is disinflation, a different phenomenon that is often confused with it."),
    q("One danger of deflation is that consumers", ["spend more in anticipation of lower prices", "postpone spending in the hope of lower prices still", "cannot borrow at all", "lose their savings to banks"], 1, "Postpone spending. If goods will be cheaper next month, waiting pays, and the resulting fall in demand deepens the very deflation that caused the waiting."),
    q("The quantity theory of money states that, other things being equal, a rise in the money supply leads to", ["a proportional rise in the price level", "a fall in the price level", "no change in prices", "a fall in output"], 0, "A proportional rise in the price level, if velocity and real output are constant. That is the classical statement of the link between money and prices."),
    q("Which measure is used to track inflation in Nigeria?", ["the consumer price index", "the human development index", "the balance of payments", "the exchange rate"], 0, "The consumer price index follows the cost of a representative basket, and its percentage change is the headline inflation rate."),
    q("A rise in the policy rate is intended to", ["encourage borrowing and spending", "discourage borrowing and reduce aggregate demand", "increase government spending", "lower the exchange rate"], 1, "Discourage borrowing: dearer credit reduces investment and consumption. It raises rather than lowers the currency's attraction, and it has nothing to do with government spending."),
    q("Near money is best described as", ["notes and coins in circulation", "assets that can be converted into cash quickly with little loss", "money held in foreign accounts", "currency that is no longer legal tender"], 1, "Assets convertible into cash quickly and at little cost — treasury bills, savings deposits. Notes and coins are money proper rather than near money."),
    q("The money supply is likely to rise when", ["the central bank sells securities", "the reserve ratio is increased", "commercial banks lend more", "the public holds more cash outside the banks"], 2, "More lending creates more deposits, which is more money. Selling securities, raising the reserve ratio and an increased preference for cash all reduce it."),
    q("A commercial bank's main source of profit is the", ["difference between the interest it pays depositors and charges borrowers", "profit from printing currency", "revenue from treasury bill purchases", "fee for holding government accounts"], 0, "The spread between deposit and lending rates. Printing currency is the central bank's monopoly and holding government accounts is its function."),
    q("Bank runs are dangerous because banks", ["hold long-term loans against short-term deposits", "hold all deposits in cash", "lend only to the government", "cannot create credit"], 0, "They borrow short and lend long, so no bank could repay every depositor at once. A run destroys a solvent bank by demanding that it do exactly that."),
    q("Which is an example of an asset that is highly liquid but a poor store of value over a long period?", ["a plot of land", "cash in a current account", "a ten-year bond", "a factory building"], 1, "Cash. It can be spent instantly, which is liquidity, but inflation erodes its purchasing power over years, which is the store-of-value function failing."),
    q("If the reserve ratio falls from 25 per cent to 20 per cent, the credit multiplier", ["falls from 5 to 4", "rises from 4 to 5", "stays at 5", "falls to 0.2"], 1, "It rises from 4 (1 ÷ 0.25) to 5 (1 ÷ 0.20). A lower ratio leaves more of each deposit available to lend, so the system can support more credit."),
    q("An economy has excess demand and rising prices. The appropriate combination of policies is", ["raise taxes, reduce spending, sell securities", "cut taxes, raise spending, buy securities", "cut taxes and sell securities", "raise spending and lower the reserve ratio"], 0, "Contractionary fiscal and monetary policy together: raise taxes, cut spending, sell securities. Every other combination contains an expansionary element that would worsen the excess demand."),
    q("A rise in the general price level that is caused by a devaluation is best described as", ["demand-pull inflation", "imported inflation", "deflation", "disinflation"], 1, "Imported inflation. A cheaper currency makes imports dearer in domestic money, which raises costs and prices — a supply-side shock arriving through the exchange rate."),
    q("Which statement about inflation and unemployment is correct?", ["the Phillips curve shows a permanent trade-off", "a short-run trade-off exists but the curve can shift", "the two always move together", "there is no relationship of any kind"], 1, "A short-run trade-off that shifts with expectations. The curve is a menu that moves, which is precisely why it is not a law."),
    q("Which of the following is a liability of a commercial bank?", ["cash in the vault", "a customer's savings deposit", "a loan to a manufacturer", "a holding of treasury bills"], 1, "A deposit is owed to the customer, so it is a liability. The other three are assets the bank owns or is owed."),
]
